5.2  GPU drivers: the kernel, Mesa and NVIDIA

TL;DR
  - A GPU driver has two halves: a kernel driver (DRM) that owns the device and
    its memory, and a user-space library that turns OpenGL or Vulkan calls into
    GPU commands.
  - Intel (i915, xe) and AMD (amdgpu) are in the kernel, and Mesa provides their
    OpenGL and Vulkan: nothing to install.
  - NVIDIA's own driver ships a kernel module and libraries that must match
    exactly. nouveau is the open driver, slower on recent cards.

      lspci -k | grep -A3 -E 'VGA|3D'       # "Kernel driver in use"
      glxinfo -B                            # OpenGL renderer (mesa-utils)
      vulkaninfo --summary

On your machine
  card0     Intel, driver i915, shows the boot screen
  Screens   eDP-1 1920x1200 · free: DP-1, HDMI-A-1
  Compute   /dev/dri/renderD128

  Loaded    4 modules; biggest: nvidia 53.7 MiB, nvidia_modeset 1.5 MiB, iwlwifi
            476.0 KiB, snd_hda_intel 60.0 KiB
  Tainted   P: proprietary module loaded
            O: out-of-tree module loaded

Pitfalls
  - "Driver/library version mismatch" after an NVIDIA update: the old module is
    still loaded. Reboot.
  - A kernel update can leave NVIDIA's module unbuilt (DKMS failed): the desktop
    falls back to a basic driver, or a black screen.
  - With Secure Boot, out-of-tree modules must be signed (enrolled MOK key) or
    the kernel refuses them (14.3).
  - llvmpipe as the OpenGL renderer means software rendering: no GPU driver is
    working.

Deep dive

  ▾ What does each half do?

    The kernel side sets screen modes, manages video memory, and schedules
    command buffers from many programs safely: one program can't read another's
    buffers. The user side, Mesa or NVIDIA's libraries, does the heavy lifting:
    compiling shaders into the GPU's instruction set and building the command
    buffers. Vulkan and OpenGL find it through loader files in
    /usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/ and glvnd.

  ▾ Why is NVIDIA different?

    NVIDIA long kept its kernel driver closed, so it can't be part of Linux and
    is built against each kernel separately. Since 2022 its kernel modules exist
    as open source (the default for recent GPUs), but the user-space libraries
    stay closed and versioned with them. Meanwhile, Mesa's NVK brings an open
    Vulkan driver for NVIDIA cards, on top of nouveau.

Related
  Commands  lspci, glxinfo, vulkaninfo, modinfo
  Paths     /sys/class/drm/, /proc/driver/nvidia/version
  See also  idk 15.2 Kernel modules
  See also  idk 5.3 How a picture reaches the screen: KMS, Wayland and X11
  See also  idk 14.2 The kernel command line
